  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🚚 Trucking Company Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a trucking, haulage or logistics firm, from Loadstar Freight to Hartley & Sons Haulage."
    },
    "intro": "Name your business with this **Trucking Company Name Generator**: for owner-operators, haulage firms, logistics companies and hotshot or box truck businesses. Pick a style and kind of work, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I check a trucking company name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I name my trucking company?",
        "answer": "Pick a name that is short enough to read on the side of a moving truck and easy to say over the phone to a broker or dispatcher. Family names (\"Hartley & Sons Haulage\"), initials (\"J&R Trucking\") and road words (\"Loadstar Freight\") are the classics. \"Trucking\" sounds American and \"Haulage\" British, while \"Transport\", \"Freight\" and \"Logistics\" work in both."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does my truck have to show the business name?",
        "answer": "In the US, trucks run under a USDOT number must show the carrier's legal name or one trade name and the USDOT number on both sides, and the name must match your FMCSA registration, so choose it before you apply for your operating authority. In the UK, goods vehicles over 3.5 tonnes need an operator's licence in the business's name. Avoid \"Royal Mail\", \"Post Office\" and \"Royal\", and keep clear of big carriers' and truck makers' names."
      }
    ]
  },
